<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
    .jwmrc - JWM configuration with dwm-like key bindings

    Based on JWM's stock configuration (example.jwmrc from JWM 2.4.6)
    with subdued colors

      active window        beige on #5d594d
      inactive windows     Gainsboro on Gray20
      bottom bar           as the windows; minimized windows Gray60
      pager                #5d594d desks, #7e7a5c for the current one
      menus                same as inactive window
      desktop background   grey15

    The key bindings follow dwm's defaults, with Alt (Mod1, dwm's own
    default) as the modifier.  JWM's nine desktops stand in for dwm's tags:

      Mod+p                dmenu_run
      Mod+Shift+Return     terminal (st)
      Mod+b                show the tray
      Mod+j / Mod+k        focus the next / previous window
      Mod+h / Mod+l        snap the window to the left / right half
      Mod+m                maximize the window (toggle)
      Mod+f                fullscreen the window (toggle)
      Mod+Shift+c          close the window
      Mod+1..9             view a desktop; again to go back to the last one
      Mod+Shift+1..9       send the window to a desktop
      Mod+Shift+0          show the window on all desktops (toggle)
      Mod+Shift+q          quit
      Mod+button1          move the window
      Mod+button3          resize the window

    JWM is a stacking window manager, so dwm's layouts have no counterpart:
    Mod+t, Mod+space and Mod+Shift+space (layouts and floating), Mod+Return
    (zoom), Mod+i and Mod+d (the master area) and Mod+0 (view all tags) are
    left unbound.  Mod+h and Mod+l resize the master area in dwm; here they
    snap a window to half of the screen.  Mod+f is the floating layout in
    dwm; here, where every window floats, it is fullscreen.  Two more
    differences: Mod+Shift+1..9 follows the window to its new desktop, as
    JWM's send action does, where dwm stays put; and pressing Mod with the
    number of the current desktop returns to the previous one
    (backandforth, below), which does the job of dwm's Mod+Tab.

    A few bindings keep JWM's own features within reach:

      Mod+Escape           the root menu
      Mod+w                the window menu
      Mod+Left / Right     the desktop to the left / right
      Mod+Shift+r          restart JWM (to reread this file)

    The clock uses "%I" rather than "%l", which musl, the C library rdesk's
    programs are built with, does not support: the clock was blank.

    JWM sets the desktop background each time it starts, and it restarts
    itself when the desktop is resized, so a colour set with xsetroot lasts
    only until the next resize.  To manage the background yourself, remove the
    Background tag below, or have JWM run your command for it:
      <Background type="command">xsetroot -solid steelblue</Background>

    Check this file with "jwm -p" before restarting JWM.

    The stock configuration is part of JWM, whose license follows.

